<article class="env-lemma" id="0BJN">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0BJN">Lemma <span data-tag="0BJN">47.23.1</span>.</a> Properties (A), (B), (C), (D), and (E) of More on Algebra, Section <a href="/tag/0BIR" data-tag="0BIR">15.52</a> hold for $P(k \to R) =$“$R$ is a Gorenstein ring”. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Since we already know the result holds for Cohen-Macaulay instead of Gorenstein, we may in each step assume the ring we have is Cohen-Macaulay. This is not particularly helpful for the proof, but psychologically may be useful. 
    <p>
      Part (A). Let $K/k$ be a finitely generated field extension. Let $R$ be a Gorenstein $k$-algebra. We can find a global complete intersection $A = k[x_1, \ldots , x_ n]/(f_1, \ldots , f_ c)$ over $k$ such that $K$ is isomorphic to the fraction field of $A$, see Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/07BV" data-tag="07BV">10.158.11</a>. Then $R \to R \otimes _ k A$ is a relative global complete intersection. Hence $R \otimes _ k A$ is Gorenstein by Lemma <a href="/tag/0DWA" data-tag="0DWA">47.21.8</a>. Thus $R \otimes _ k K$ is too as a localization. 
    <p>
      Proof of (B). This is clear because a ring is Gorenstein if and only if all of its local rings are Gorenstein. 
    <p>
      Part (C). Let $A \to B \to C$ be flat maps of Noetherian rings. Assume the fibres of $A \to B$ are Gorenstein and $B \to C$ is regular. We have to show the fibres of $A \to C$ are Gorenstein. Clearly, we may assume $A = k$ is a field. Then we may assume that $B \to C$ is a regular local homomorphism of Noetherian local rings. Then $B$ is Gorenstein and $C/\mathfrak m_ B C$ is regular, in particular Gorenstein (Lemma <a href="/tag/0AWX" data-tag="0AWX">47.21.3</a>). Then $C$ is Gorenstein by Lemma <a href="/tag/0BJL" data-tag="0BJL">47.21.9</a>. 
    <p>
      Part (D). This follows from Lemma <a href="/tag/0BJL" data-tag="0BJL">47.21.9</a>. Part (E) is immediate as the condition does not refer to the ground field. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>